  "topics": ["binomial expansion", "negative integer power"],
  "methods": ["known series for (1-x)^{-2}", "substitution x -> -3x"],
  "statement": "Use the expansion $(1-x)^{-2}=1+2x+3x^{2}+4x^{3}+\\cdots$ to find the first four terms of $(1+3x)^{-2}$.",
  "solution": "In the given series replace $x$ by $-3x$:\n\\begin{align*}\n(1+3x)^{-2}&=\\bigl(1-(-3x)\\bigr)^{-2}\\\\\n&=1+2(-3x)+3(-3x)^{2}+4(-3x)^{3}+\\cdots\\\\\n&=1-6x+3(9x^{2})+4(-27x^{3})+\\cdots\\\\\n&=1-6x+27x^{2}-108x^{3}+\\cdots.\n\\end{align*}",
  "solutionStatus": "full",
  "hints": [
    "Recognise (1+3x)^{-2} = (1 - (-3x))^{-2}.",
    "Substitute x -> -3x in the given series.",
    "Be careful with the signs of the odd powers."
  ]
}
